  You will want to take a look at the C documentation around the
  declaration and use of the string descriptor structure.  I've
  added a colon to the device, I've switched to the $DESCRIPTOR
  macro (use of the dsc$descriptor structure would have worked),
  I've changed to the correct case -- lowercase -- for the call
  to the sys$assign() routine, I've added a few include files, and
  I've switched over to the status check macro $VMS_STATUS_SUCCESS.
  I've also changed a few variable names to reflect their use...

	--

  #include <descrip.h>
  #include <starlet.h>
  #include <stsdef.h>

  unsigned short int InputChan;
  int Status;
  $DESCRIPTOR( InputDevDescr, "SYS$INPUT:" );

  Status = sys$assign( &InputDevDescr, &InputChan, 0, 0);
  if (!$VMS_STATUS_SUCCESS( Status ))
      return Status;

	--

  If you choose to use a string descriptor and are manually
  initializing it, you will want to use the descriptor class
  DSC$K_CLASS_S until you fully understand the declaration and
  use of the dynamic string descriptor class DSC$K_CLASS_D. 
  And most common string descriptors use the text string data
  type: DSC$K_DTYPE_T.  (These values, and the structure of
  various string descriptors are declared in descrip.h.)

	--

  I strongly recommend taking a look at the DEC C manuals, as they
  cover various topics related to the use of DEC C on OpenVMS.  (If
  you are still using VAX C, seriously consider upgrading to DEC C.)


