     The descriptor he is using is not the source of his problem,
because is it completely correct (as long as `int' and `char *' are
each 32 bits, and assuming that the length value fits within 16 bits,
so as not to overflow into the type & class fields).  Probably 98%
of VAX/VMS assembler code--including within VMS itself--would have
constructed a similar descriptor without bothering with the type
and class.

     System services do not even examine the type and class fields.
Even if they did, 0 is a valid value (indicating "unspecified", which
is treated as static for the class and arbitrary bytes for the type).
His descriptor did have zeroes in those two fields due to the bit
pattern of the length value 9.

     Neither adding a trailing colon to "SYS$INPUT" nor changing
the case of "SYS$ASSIGN" makes any difference.  Almost certainly the
original problem was due to his actual translation of "SYS$INPUT",
not to the code which was posted.

		Pat Rankin, rankin@eql.caltech.edu



